1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:07,710
Uh, today we are looking at evaluating vendor claims of software being, you know, GovStack compliant for government digital systems.

2
00:00:08,270 --> 00:00:14,409
Right. Because just hearing a vendor say their product is GovStack compliant, well, that tells you very little on its own.

3
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:19,210
Exactly. So our mission today is helping officials who run ministry programs

4
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:26,550
move past those initial vendor claims, uh, to demand hard, verifiable evidence for their digital infrastructure.

5
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:34,330
Yeah. True evaluation requires actually taking that product and checking it against the published specifications, requirement by requirement.

6
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:40,250
So if you are a director general trying to avoid fragmented or duplicated systems in your ministry,

7
00:00:41,110 --> 00:00:46,350
how do we ensure every single vendor bidding on a tender is answering to the exact same standard?

8
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:52,050
Well, it starts by naming the exact default edition of the reference architecture in your tenders and your questionnaires.

9
00:00:52,230 --> 00:00:54,070
Ooh, like a specific version number.

10
00:00:54,590 --> 00:00:56,690
Right, such as edition twenty-three Q four.

11
00:00:56,950 --> 00:01:02,509
By writing that exact edition into your documents, uh, you ensure all answers refer to the very same text.

12
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:04,790
Okay, so they all have the exact same baseline.

13
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:07,210
What does that specification actually consist of?

14
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:16,110
It is quite concrete. There are forty-two functional requirements which are strictly divided among the applicant, the operator, and the system analyst.

15
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:19,770
That makes sense, keeping those user roles completely distinct.

16
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:27,630
Exactly. And it also includes twelve data structures, you know, things like the service, the registration, and the role, plus thirteen published operations.

17
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:29,950
Are all of those mandatory for the vendor?

18
00:01:30,730 --> 00:01:34,310
Well, each requirement is strictly marked as either required or recommended.

19
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:37,570
Okay, but a list of forty-two requirements is great on paper.

20
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:45,390
How do we prevent vendors from just saying yes to everything, leading to a system where your program ends up locked in to one supplier?

21
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:48,330
That is where the mandatory response format comes in.

22
00:01:48,830 --> 00:01:56,710
Vendors cannot just say yes. They must answer if a requirement is met as delivered, met by configuration, or not met.

23
00:01:57,110 --> 00:01:58,750
Wait, met by configuration.

24
00:01:59,570 --> 00:02:03,490
Doesn't that just mean they have to build it from scratch, and it becomes costly to change later?

25
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:08,310
You would think so, but crucially, every single answer requires verifiable evidence.

26
00:02:08,690 --> 00:02:10,490
Oh, they actually have to prove it up front.

27
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:15,330
Yes, exactly. Your team demands to see a screen, a test record, or a document.

28
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:24,190
They also must specify exactly which of the thirteen operations they offer and, uh, prove how they handle importing and exporting service descriptions.

29
00:02:24,590 --> 00:02:28,350
So if I am forcing a vendor to do all this granular homework,

30
00:02:29,170 --> 00:02:34,870
does GovStack actually grade it? I mean, do they test your ministry's specific installation to make sure it runs?

31
00:02:35,510 --> 00:02:37,870
No, and that is a very common misconception.

32
00:02:38,270 --> 00:02:43,790
GovStack itself provides a self-assessment form for the software provider alongside automated tests.

33
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:47,290
Oh, so it is self-reported but run through their tests.

34
00:02:47,790 --> 00:02:51,570
Right. GovStack's team checks that submission for completeness and plausibility.

35
00:02:52,030 --> 00:03:00,290
They ultimately list products at level one or level two compliance, but, uh, this is just a review of what the provider submitted, not a test of your local installation.

36
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:02,990
That clarifies things quite a bit. Mm-hmm.

37
00:03:03,270 --> 00:03:06,410
I know we have the demonstration country Progressa as an example here.

38
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:09,610
Yeah. Progressa shows exactly how this works in practice.

39
00:03:10,070 --> 00:03:15,850
So for a requirement like making decisions, the vendor answered that it was met by configuration.

40
00:03:16,070 --> 00:03:17,430
And they provided the evidence for that.

41
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:24,290
Exactly, via a screenshot of the actual registrar screen showing the specific buttons to approve or reject.

42
00:03:24,910 --> 00:03:27,390
What about features that a program just does not use?

43
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:29,930
Well, take the requirement for paying fees.

44
00:03:30,490 --> 00:03:36,410
In the case of Progressa, this was marked as met but not used simply because learner registration carries no fee.

45
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:37,410
Oh, I see.

46
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:44,430
So it forces the vendor to map out the reality of the software, avoiding any rekeyed or siloed data down the line.

